'''Bijolia Parshvanath Temple''' or '''Tapodaya Teerth Kshetra''' is a [[Jain]] [[tirtha (Jainism)|pilgrimage center]] located in [[Bijolia]] town in [[Bhilwara district]] of [[Rajasthan]].

== History ==
[[File:Bijolia Parasnath 33.jpg|thumb|left|Bijolia inscription]]

Bijolia is famous for two rock inscriptions, both dated 1170 CE ([[Vikram Samvat|V. S.]] 1226).{{sfn|Carlleyle|1878|p=239}} One inscription provides the genealogy of the [[Chahamanas of Shakambhari]] and the second is a Jaina poem called {{lang|sa|Uttama Sikhara Purana}}. The first inscription opens with salutation to [[Parshvanatha]] and records coronation of [[Someshvara (Chahamana dynasty)|Somesvara]].{{sfn|Majumdar|1956|p=109}} The third verse of inscription describes how Someshvara gave the grant to build Parshvanatha temple in Rewna village.{{sfn|Mishra|1973|p=127}}{{sfn|Kapoor|2002|p=156}}{{sfn|Bakshi|2005|p=341}} The latter inscription, engraved near the door of the Parsvanatha temple, records the homage of Manoratha, son of Mahidhara.{{sfn|Aufrecht|1896|p=27}} According to Jain tradition, this place is believed to be where the Uttama Sikhara Purana was composed.{{sfn|Royal Asiatic Society of Great Britain and Ireland|1906|p=700}}{{sfn|Joshi|Deva|2006|p=65}}{{sfn|Sircar|2017|p=71}}{{sfn|ASI}}

According to Peter Flügel, the Undeshvar Shiva temple of Bijolia was originally a [[Śvētāmbara]] temple based on carvings of prominent Jain figures in the temple exterior.{{sfn|Flügel|2006|p=402}}

== About temple ==
Five Jain temples was constructed in 1160 CE by Mahajan Lala during the reign of King Somesvara of Chahamanas empire. The main temple is a large structure consisting of a small model of the temple.{{sfn|Archaeological Survey of India|1904|p=8}} The temple is dedicated to Parshvanatha and is a major Jain pilgrimage center.{{sfn|Carlleyle|1878|p=239}}{{sfn|Kanakk|2020}}

